\fs96 \cf2 Value destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As soon as a value/object is no longer used, Mojo destroys it. Mojo does not wait until the end of a code block\'97or even until the end of an expression\'97to destroy an unused value. It destroys values using an "as soon as possible" (ASAP) destruction policy that runs after every sub-expression. Even within an expression like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a+b+c+d
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Mojo destroys the intermediate values as soon as they're no longer needed.\
Mojo uses static analysis at compile-time to determine the last use of a value. At that point, it immediately ends the value's lifetime and calls the implicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  destructor. You can override 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in your structs to perform any required cleanup.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 For types that require stronger guarantees, Mojo also supports explicit compiler-checked destructors, which provide an additional layer of safety for managing cleanup. For details, read about \ul explicit destruction\ulnone  below.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For example, notice when the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  destructor is called for each instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloon
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Balloon\cf5 (\cf15 Writable\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  color\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 write_to\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf5 mut\cf15  writer\cf5 :\cf15  Some\cf5 [\cf15 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf15 String\cf5 (\cf17 "a "\cf5 ,\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 color\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 " balloon"\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Destroyed"\cf5 ,\cf15  String\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  a \cf5 =\cf15  Balloon\cf5 (\cf17 "red"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  b \cf5 =\cf15  Balloon\cf5 (\cf17 "blue"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 a\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # a.__del__() runs here for "red" Balloon\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     a \cf5 =\cf15  Balloon\cf5 (\cf17 "green"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # a.__del__() runs immediately because "green" Balloon is never used\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 b\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # b.__del__() runs here\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 a red balloon\cb1 \
\cb14 Destroyed a red balloon\cb1 \
\cb14 Destroyed a green balloon\cb1 \
\cb14 a blue balloon\cb1 \
\cb14 Destroyed a blue balloon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice that each initialization of a value is matched with a call to the destructor, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 a
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is actually destroyed multiple times\'97once for each time it receives a new value.\
Also notice that this 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  implementation doesn't actually do anything. Most structs don't require a custom destructor, and Mojo automatically adds a no-op destructor if you don't define one.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method takes its argument using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}}, which indicates that the value is being deinitialized.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Default destruction behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You may be wondering how Mojo can destroy a type without a custom destructor, or why a no-op destructor is useful. If a type is simply a collection of fields, like the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloon
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example, Mojo only needs to destroy the fields: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloon
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't dynamically allocate memory or use any long-lived resources (like file handles). There's no special action to take when a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloon
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is destroyed.\
When a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloon
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is destroyed, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value in its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 color
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field is no longer used, and it is also immediately destroyed.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is a little more complicated. Mojo strings are mutable. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object has an internal buffer\'97a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 List}} field, which holds the characters that make up the string. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  stores its contents in dynamically allocated memory on the heap, so the string can grow or shrink. The string itself doesn't have any special destructor logic, but when Mojo destroys a string, it calls the destructor for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field, which de-allocates the memory.\
Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't require any custom destructor logic, it has a no-op destructor: literally, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that doesn't do anything. This may seem pointless, but it means that Mojo can call the destructor on any value when its lifetime ends. This makes it easier to write generic containers and algorithms.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Benefits of ASAP destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Similar to other languages, Mojo follows the principle that objects/values acquire resources in a constructor (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and release resources in a destructor (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). However, Mojo's ASAP destruction has some advantages over scope-based destruction (such as the C++ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/raii"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 RAII pattern}}, which waits until the end of the code scope to destroy values):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Destroying values immediately at last-use composes nicely with the "move" optimization, which transforms a "copy+del" pair into a "move" operation.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Destroying values at end-of-scope in C++ is problematic for some common patterns like tail recursion, because the destructor call happens after the tail call. This can be a significant performance and memory problem for certain functional programming patterns, which is not a problem in Mojo, because the destructor call always happens before the tail call.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Mojo destruction policy is more similar to how Rust and Swift work, because they both have strong value ownership tracking and provide memory safety. One difference is that Rust and Swift require the use of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/drop-flags.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 dynamic "drop flag"}}\'97they maintain hidden shadow variables to keep track of the state of your values to provide safety. These are often optimized away, but the Mojo approach eliminates this overhead entirely, making the generated code faster and avoiding ambiguity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Destructors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo calls a value's destructor (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method) when the value's lifetime ends (typically the point at which the value is last used). As we mentioned earlier, Mojo provides a default, no-op destructor for all types, so in most cases you don't need to define the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method.\
You should define the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to perform any kind of cleanup the type requires. Usually, that includes freeing memory for any fields where you dynamically allocated memory (for example, via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and closing any long-lived resources such as file handles.\
However, any struct that is just a simple collection of other types does not need to implement the destructor.\
For example, consider this simple struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Balloons\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  color\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  count\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 There's no need to define the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  destructor for this, because it's a simple collection of other types (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), and it doesn't dynamically allocate memory.\
Whereas, the following struct must define the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to free the memory allocated by its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UnsafePointer}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 HeapArray\cf5 (\cf15 Writable\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  data\cf5 :\cf15  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ,\cf15  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  size\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf15  self\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf5 *\cf15 values\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 size \cf5 =\cf15  \cf16 len\cf5 (\cf15 values\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 data \cf5 =\cf15  alloc\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ](\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 size\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 for\cf15  i \cf5 in\cf15  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 size\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 data \cf5 +\cf15  i\cf5 ).\cf15 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf15 values\cf5 [\cf15 i\cf5 ])\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 write_to\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf5 mut\cf15  writer\cf5 :\cf15  Some\cf5 [\cf15 Writer\cf5 ]):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf17 "["\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 for\cf15  i \cf5 in\cf15  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 size\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             writer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 data\cf5 [\cf15 i\cf5 ])\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 if\cf15  i \cf5 <\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 size \cf5 -\cf15  \cf20 1\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14                 writer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf17 ", "\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         writer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf17 "]"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf17 "Destroying"\cf5 ,\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 size\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 "elements"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 for\cf15  i \cf5 in\cf15  \cf16 range\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 size\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 data \cf5 +\cf15  i\cf5 ).\cf15 destroy_pointee\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 data\cf5 .\cf15 free\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  a \cf5 =\cf15  HeapArray\cf5 (\cf20 10\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf20 1\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf20 3\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf20 9\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 a\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 [10, 1, 3, 9]\cb1 \
\cb14 Destroying 4 elements\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The destructor takes its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}}, which grants exclusive ownership of the value and marks it as destroyed at the end of the function. (For more information on the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Field lifetimes during move and destruction\cf2 \ulnone ).\
Note that a pointer doesn't own any values in the memory it points to, so when a pointer is destroyed, Mojo doesn't call the destructors on those values.\
So in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 HeapArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example above, calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 free()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on the pointer releases the memory, but doesn't call the destructors on the stored values. To invoke the destructors, use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 destroy_pointee()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method provided by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb13 You shouldn't call the destructor explicitly. If you need to ensure that a destructor is called at a specific point, use the discard pattern described in \ul explicit lifetime extension\ulnone .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 It's important to notice that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is an "extra" cleanup event, and your implementation does not override any default destruction behaviors. For example, Mojo still destroys all the fields in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Balloons
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  even if you add a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that to do nothing:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Balloons\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  color\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  count\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Mojo destroys all the fields when they're last used\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 pass\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value inside the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  destructor is still whole (so all fields are still usable) until the destructor returns, as we'll discuss more in the following section.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Explicitly-destroyed types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports both implicit and explicit value destruction. Each approach balances different needs around safety, performance, and overhead.\
One way to think about explicitly destroyed types is that they create a future requirement, a promise of action. Creating an instance commits you to performing specific actions (for example, flushing, closing, committing, or discarding) when the value has no further uses.\
This ability to "control the future" is a powerful tool for developers. It allows teardown requirements to be added directly into the type system. It guides users toward valid cleanup paths, making programs that skip those paths impossible to compile and elevating safe destruction to a first-class part of a value's lifecycle.\
With implicit destruction, the compiler manages cleanup. It automatically calls 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when a value has no further uses, based on lifetime analysis. You do not invoke the destructor yourself.\
With explicit destruction, you take over cleanup responsibilities. You must define and call a named cleanup method (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cleanup()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 save_and_close()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) and you must call that cleanup method before the end of the scope in which your value lives. The compiler disables automatic destruction. It requires you to consume the value. Failing to do so results in a compiler error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Most types use implicit destruction. When a value's lifetime ends, Mojo automatically calls 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to clean up. This works for most cases: memory is freed and simple resources are released automatically.\
Some types need more control. A buffered file writer must flush before closing. A transaction must commit or roll back. A lock must be released explicitly with proper error handling. These operations may fail, require a specific order, or depend on deliberate decisions about how cleanup occurs.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator disables automatic destruction. Instead, you define named destructor methods that must be called intentionally. The compiler enforces this requirement: failing to destroy the value produces a compile-time error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Building explicit destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To build explicit destruction, define a method that takes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument and decorate the type with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This enables compile-time checks for your deinitializers and gives you space to add actions that must be performed before destruction.\
Unlike the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, named explicit destructors can raise errors. This gives you control over how an instance is deallocated. If a destructor raises an error, the call stack can return to logic that selects an alternative cleanup path, such as invoking a secondary destructor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @explicit_destroy(\cf17 "Must call save_and_close() or discard()"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 FileBuffer\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  path\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  data\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf15  self\cf5 ,\cf15  path\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 path \cf5 =\cf15  path\cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 data \cf5 =\cf15  \cf17 ""\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 write\cf5 (mut\cf15  self\cf5 ,\cf15  content\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 data \cf5 +=\cf15  content\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf6 # Choose one cleanup path from these two options\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 save_and_close\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 )\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         write_to_disk\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 path\cf5 ,\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 data\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 discard\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Abandon buffered data without writing\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 pass\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When used, you must invoke one of the destructors:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 write_log\cf5 (\cf15 path\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ,\cf15  message\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 )\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  \cf16 buffer\cf15  \cf5 =\cf15  FileBuffer\cf5 (\cf15 path\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 buffer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf15 message\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 buffer\cf5 ^.\cf15 save_and_close\cf5 ()\cf15   \cf6 # Required: explicit destruction\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The compiler verifies that a destructor is called for each explicitly destroyed value and emits an error if it cannot find one. If you supply a custom message to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , that message appears in the error output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 broken_write\cf5 (\cf15 path\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ,\cf15  message\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  \cf16 buffer\cf15  \cf5 =\cf15  FileBuffer\cf5 (\cf15 path\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 buffer\cf5 .\cf15 write\cf5 (\cf15 message\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # ERROR: error: 'buffer' abandoned without being explicitly destroyed:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 #                Must call save_and_close() or discard()\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
