\fs48 \cf2 When to use explicit destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when cleanup cannot be handled automatically and must be controlled in code. Common cases include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cleanup can fail and requires error handling.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Multiple cleanup paths are possible.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The order of cleanup operations matters.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cleanup is expensive and should be deliberate.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Multiple cleanup paths\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 @explicit_destroy(\cf17 "Use commit() or rollback()"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Transaction\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 commit\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 )\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15  \cf6 # Requires error handling\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 ...\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 rollback\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 ...\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # Order matters\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 @explicit_destroy(\cf17 "Use unlock()"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 MutexGuard\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Must be called to release the lock before other operations\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 unlock\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 ...\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # Cleanup is expensive\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 @explicit_destroy(\cf17 "Use finalize() to commit expensive computation"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 BatchProcessor\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Expensive operation user should explicitly trigger\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 execute\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 )\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 ...\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Explicit destruction in generic code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Generic code parameterized over 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can accept both implicitly and explicitly destructible values. However, it cannot automatically destroy values that require explicit destruction.\
Because explicit destructors are type-specific, generic code has no way to name or invoke them unless the type is constrained:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  generic_function_1\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  AnyType\cf5 ](var\cf15  value\cf5 :\cf15  T\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # ERROR: 'value' abandoned without being explicitly destroyed:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 #        Unhandled explicitly destroyed type AnyType\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 pass\cf15   \cf6 # value abandoned here\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  generic_function_2\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ](var\cf15  value\cf5 :\cf15  T\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # OK: T can be implicitly destroyed\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 pass\cf15   \cf6 # value.__del__() called automatically\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If a generic function may consume its argument, it must ensure that the value can be safely destroyed at the end of its lifetime. Generic code has no way to invoke type-specific explicit destructors unless that behavior is constrained. Choose one of the following approaches:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Accept the value by mutable or immutable reference instead of by ownership, so the function does not consume it.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Return the value or transfer ownership instead of consuming it.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Require implicit destruction with the ImplicitlyDestructible trait, which guarantees that a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  destructor is available and can be called automatically:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  generic_function_3\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  ImplicitlyDestructible\cf5 ](var\cf15  value\cf5 :\cf15  T\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf15 \cb14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf5 pass\cf15   \cf6 # value.__del__() called automatically\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf15 \cb14 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf18 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In a method, adding the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  means the method fully consumes the value by the time it returns. Specifically, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  signals that:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The method takes ownership of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
No automatic destructor is called after the method returns.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fields may be transferred or explicitly destroyed inside the method.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Additional cleanup work may take place within the method.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The value is considered destroyed when the method completes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @explicit_destroy\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Example\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  field\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 consume_example\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf5 mut\cf15  other\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Can transfer field values\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         other\cf5 .\cf15 field \cf5 =\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 field\cf5 ^\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # No automatic destructor called\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # self is considered destroyed when method returns\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Methods marked with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can transfer ownership of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods. Chaining deinitialization methods allows cleanup responsibilities to be delegated:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 cleanup_method1\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     self\cf5 ^.\cf15 cleanup_method2\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 cleanup_method2\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Actually perform the cleanup\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 pass\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
