\fs64 \cf2 Field lifetimes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In addition to tracking the lifetime of all objects in a program, Mojo also tracks each field of a structure independently. That is, Mojo keeps track of whether a "whole object" is fully or partially initialized/destroyed, and it destroys each field independently with its ASAP destruction policy.\
For example, consider this code that changes the value of a field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Balloons\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  color\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  count\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  balloons \cf5 =\cf15  Balloons\cf5 (\cf17 "red"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf20 5\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 balloons\cf5 .\cf15 color\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # balloons.color.__del__() runs here, because this instance is\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # no longer used; it's replaced below\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     balloons\cf5 .\cf15 color \cf5 =\cf15  \cf17 "blue"\cf15   \cf6 # Overwrite balloons.color\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 balloons\cf5 .\cf15 color\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # balloons.__del__() runs here\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 balloons.color
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field is destroyed after the first 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , because Mojo knows that it will be overwritten below. You can also see this behavior when using the transfer sigil:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 consume\cf5 (var\cf15  arg\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 pass\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 use\cf5 (\cf15 arg\cf5 :\cf15  Balloons\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 arg\cf5 .\cf15 count\cf5 ,\cf15  arg\cf5 .\cf15 color\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 "balloons."\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 consume_and_use\cf5 ():\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  balloons \cf5 =\cf15  Balloons\cf5 (\cf17 "blue"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf20 8\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     consume\cf5 (\cf15 balloons\cf5 .\cf15 color\cf5 ^)\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # String(take=) runs here, which invalidates balloons.color\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Now balloons is only partially initialized\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf6 # use(balloons)  # This fails because balloons.color is uninitialized\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     balloons\cf5 .\cf15 color \cf5 =\cf15  String\cf5 (\cf17 "orange"\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # All together now\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     use\cf5 (\cf15 balloons\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # This is ok\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # balloons.__del__() runs here (and only if the object is whole)\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Notice that the code transfers ownership of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 consume()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For a period of time after that, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  field is uninitialized. Then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is reinitialized before it is passed to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 use()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function. If you try calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 use()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  before 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is re-initialized, Mojo rejects the code with an uninitialized field error.\
Also, if you don't re-initialize the name by the end of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  lifetime, the compiler complains because it's unable to destroy a partially initialized object.\
Mojo's policy here is powerful and intentionally straight-forward: fields can be temporarily transferred, but the "whole object" must be constructed with the aggregate type's initializer and destroyed with the aggregate destructor. This means it's impossible to create an object by initializing only its fields, and it's likewise impossible to destroy an object by destroying only its fields.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Field lifetimes during move and destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Both the consuming move constructor and the destructor take their operand with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention. This grants exclusive ownership of the value and marks it as destroyed at the end of the function. Within the function body, Mojo's ASAP policy still applies to fields: each field is destroyed immediately after its last use.\
Just to recap, the move constructor and destructor method signatures look like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 TwoStrings\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __init__\cf5 (out\cf15  self\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf5 *,\cf15  \cf5 deinit\cf15  take\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Initializes a new `self` by consuming the contents of `take`\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Destroys all resources in `self`\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Like the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention gives the argument exclusive ownership of a value. But unlike a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, Mojo doesn't insert a destructor call for the argument at the end of the function\'97because the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention already marks it as a value that's in the process of being destroyed.\
For example, the following code shows how fields are destroyed inside a destructor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 consume\cf5 (var\cf15  \cf16 str\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf17 "Consumed"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf16 str\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 TwoStrings\cf5 (\cf15 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  str1\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  str2\cf5 :\cf15  String\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # self value is whole at the beginning of the function\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 .\cf15 dump\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # After dump(): str2 is never used again, so str2.__del__() runs now\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14         consume\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 str1\cf5 ^)\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # self.str1 has been transferred so str1 becomes uninitialized, and\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # no destructor is called for str1.\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # self.__del__() is not called (avoiding an infinite loop).\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 dump\cf5 (mut\cf15  self\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf17 "str1:"\cf5 ,\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 str1\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf17 "str2:"\cf5 ,\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 str2\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 main\cf5 ()\cf15  raises\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  two_strings \cf5 =\cf15  TwoStrings\cf5 (\cf17 "foo"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 "bar"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

