\fs64 \cf2 Explicit lifetime extension\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Most of the time, Mojo's ASAP destruction "just works." Very rarely, you may need to explicitly mark the last use of a value to control when its destructor runs. Think of this as an explicit last-use marker for the lifetime checker, not a general-purpose pattern.\
You might do this:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When writing tests that intentionally create otherwise-unused values (to avoid warnings or dead-code elimination).\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When writing unsafe/advanced code (for example, code that manipulates a value's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul origin}}).\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When you need deterministic destructor timing relative to specific side effects (such as logging or profiling).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mark the last use by assigning the value to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  discard pattern at the point where it is okay to destroy it. This sets the last use at that line, so the destructor runs immediately after the statement:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Without explicit extension: s is last used in the print() call, so it is\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb14 # destroyed immediately afterwards.\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  s \cf5 =\cf15  \cf17 "abc"\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 s\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # s.__del__() runs after this line\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # With explicit extension: push last-use to the discard line.\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  t \cf5 =\cf15  \cf17 "xyz"\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 t\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # ... some time later\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf15 \cb14 _ \cf5 =\cf15  t  \cf6 # t.__del__() runs after this line\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

